//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//
// An outlined region that computes a matmul is labelled as such, so a plugin can
// route it to a vendor kernel instead of inferring the operation from buffer
// shapes (#325). Shapes cannot answer it: for square operands every operand
// assignment conforms, and swapping them yields a plausible matrix of wrong
// numbers rather than a failure.
//
// The label rides on both ops -- `vx.kernel` names the outlined body, and
// `vx.launch` is what lowers to the dispatch call, so that is the one that has
// to carry the fact to a runtime.
//
// `c = a @ b` emits a zero-fill of the accumulator followed by the matmul, which
// is GEMM with beta = 0, so both are subsumed by one library call and the region
// still classifies. `kernel_kind_unrecognized.vx` covers the other direction.
//
// The pass pipeline is pinned to the vx lowering alone: the attribute is created
// there, and the later conversion to a dispatch call consumes it, so a default
// pipeline would show nothing.
//
// The runner matches CHECK lines as ordered substrings, so these follow the
// emission order: the launch inside the function, then the outlined kernel at
// module level.
//
// The roles are what actually resolve the ambiguity: naming the operation tells
// a plugin it has a GEMM, not which buffer is which, and for square operands no
// shape can settle it. `out` names the *slot* the kernel stores its result
// pointer through, since `c = a @ b` allocates the result inside the kernel
// rather than filling a buffer it was handed -- hence `outkind = "slot"`.
//
//
// The role indices moved when the outliner stopped capturing constants: the
// launch used to carry three memrefs and three `arith.constant`s -- the loop
// bounds and the zero-fill value -- so the output landed at operand 5. Those
// are rematerialized inside the kernel now, the launch carries only the three
// buffers, and the roles index into that. The mapping is what matters, not the
// numbers: they say which operand is A, B and the destination, and
// tests/integration_test/gemm_plan_test.rs decodes the payload and checks it
// describes the GEMM the program wrote.
// RUN: vxc %s --emit-mlir -X mlir=--pass-pipeline="builtin.module(convert-vx-to-standard)" 2>&1 | FileCheck %s
// CHECK: vx.launch @vx_npu_kernel_0
// CHECK: vx.kernel_kind = "matmul"
// CHECK: vx.kernel_out_kind = "slot"
// CHECK: vx.kernel_roles = "a:0,b:1,out:2"
// CHECK: vx.kernel @vx_npu_kernel_0
// CHECK: vx.kernel_kind = "matmul"
// CHECK: vx.kernel_out_kind = "slot"
// CHECK: vx.kernel_roles = "a:0,b:1,out:2"
//
// The label has to survive to the dispatch call or it cannot reach a plugin.
// It travels in the payload: a NUL-separated blob whose first entry is the
// kernel name, so reading it as a `const char *` still yields the name and a
// consumer predating the extension is unaffected. `payload_size` -- previously
// passed as 0 -- bounds the walk.
//
// The kernel name is left out of this check so it does not depend on the
// global kernel counter; the NUL-separated entry is what matters.
//
// RUN: vxc %s --emit-llvm 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=PAYLOAD
// PAYLOAD: \00kind=matmul\00roles=a:0,b:1,out:2\00outkind=slot\00

fn gemm(a : DynTensor<f32>, b : DynTensor<f32>) -> DynTensor<f32> {
  let mut c = Tensor<f32, [2, 3]>::uninit();
  spawn on(Topology::GPU) {
    c = a @b;
  }
  return c;
}
